The Proposals run is one clause wearing four tabs (Q748-Q752)

Ed, 2026-08-23: this should be one clause - maybe two - with the tabs in
a stack. Four one-thought paragraphs opened the Proposals section, two
pairs of them saying one thing twice, and four full-height tabs claiming
four decisions where there is one topic.

Now one paragraph, data-para="begin", carrying the pile. proposalsClause()
composes three sentences from per-decision fragments, so no gate loses its
own variants: sentence 1 is the two canPropose() branches joined to the
judge gate, sentence 2 is what it takes to pass with the voice grant's
clause on its tail, sentence 3 is the deviation baseline verbatim. All four
card heads are that clause (F15). SURFACE gains Y23 and spec-check asserts
PROPOSAL_CHIPS against the band prose's glyph run.

Measured: 0.0px on both axes for the front mark on open and for each of the
four reached within the strip, at 1600 and 1280 - including the path nothing
had exercised, a non-front chip opened from its rail entry. The four-chip
pile keeps its 4px peek with 93px of clearance below.

Two adjustments to the plan, both from the tree it was not written against.
The numbers are 748-752, claimed for this plan in QUESTIONS.md on 2026-08-24;
the plan asks for 740-744, which were spent by plans 07 and 09. And the
voice grant stays out of SEC.rate.keys, where the plan's edit list put it:
secLive() makes a section live when one of its keys is visible, and the
grant is visible from ORDER row 22, so admitting it would pull the whole
section forward and offer its news before the begin card - the F9 situation
Q745 closed the day before. The clause is pinned by chipsFor instead, one
predicate decides whether the grant is a fragment or a paragraph of its own,
tasksFor is untouched so Q605's news still travels, and spec-check takes an
explicit hosts override. voiceHost, firstAsked and beginOffered are all
untouched, as plan 09's three assertions require.

anchorOf resolved a rail entry through tabFor, and an inert chip carries no
data-tab - so a non-front stacked tab's entry fell through to #titlepara.
Nothing had reached that branch before this group. Every chip now carries
data-chip and hostParaOf consults it, for every pile.

